  This file is a scaffold. Before making the repository public or depositing
  it on Zenodo, confirm BOTH of the following and then delete this header:

  1. WHO HOLDS THE COPYRIGHT.
     Per the University of Bristol's guidance, software written by a member of
     staff as part of their work — or using University equipment — is owned by
     the University, regardless of whether it was written in work hours.
     Students (including PhD students) normally own their own copyright unless
     funding terms say otherwise or the work derives from staff-developed code.
     Only the copyright holder may choose the licence.

  2. WHETHER A GRANT ALREADY DICTATES THE LICENCE.
     Grants commonly contain a promise to release under an open-source licence,
     sometimes a specific one. That promise is contractual and overrides
     preference.

  Contacts:
     ask-rse@bristol.ac.uk   — ACRC Research Software Engineering team
     RED (Research & Enterprise Development) — Open Source Software Licensing FAQ
     https://www.bristol.ac.uk/acrc/research-software-engineering/software-howtos/how-to-license-software/

  The MIT text below is the LIKELY choice for a GROMACS-adjacent analysis tool
  (permissive, standard in the field, imposes no constraint on users), but it
  is UNCONFIRMED. Replace <YEAR> and <COPYRIGHT HOLDER> once decided.

  Note on GROMACS: GroMax invokes `gmx` as an external subprocess and does not
  link against GROMACS libraries, so GROMACS's LGPL does not propagate to this
  code and does not constrain the licence choice. State this in the README.

